Aira stepped forward, her hands steady as the rock’s pulse. She chose to share the water she kept in clay jars, letting every neighbor take a drink. The drought loosened its grip, not by magic, but by the simple act of trust. The Stone glowed faintly, then dimmed, as if satisfied.

From that day, the valley learned to listen to echoes not as warnings, but as seeds. And when the rain finally fell, the Stone of Echoes kept its vigil, reminding all who passed that futures are born from acts of grace we choose in the present.
